\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Temporal reflection positivity}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{For the periodic law of \ref{fdip-00GX}, take #{N=2m},
#{m\ge1}. The \vocabk{link reflection}{fdip-007M} is
#{\theta(j)=1-j} modulo #{2m}, with positive half
#{\{1,\ldots,m\}}. Let #{\mathcal A_+} be finite sums of
products #{F(q)=\prod_{j=1}^m f_j(q_j)}, with arbitrary bounded
measurable functions of a whole spatial slice. For a product set
##{A_F=T_\epsilon^{1/2}M_{f_1}T_\epsilon M_{f_2}\cdots
 T_\epsilon M_{f_m}T_\epsilon^{1/2},}
with just #{M_{f_1}} between the half-powers if #{m=1}, and
extend linearly. Then
##{\mathbb E_{\mu_{\epsilon,2m}}
 [\overline{F(\theta q)}F(q)]
 =\frac{\|A_F\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2}{Z_{\epsilon,2m}}\ge0.}
The \vocabk{site reflection}{fdip-007M} #{\theta(j)=-j} also
has this positivity, with positive-half observables depending on
#{q_0,\ldots,q_m}. Both assertions extend to positive-half
#{L^2} observables, including polynomials.}

\proof{
\p{By \ref{fdip-00GW}, #{T_\epsilon^{1/2}} is Hilbert–Schmidt;
all other factors are bounded. The proof of \ref{fdip-0086}
uses exactly these hypotheses, positivity of #{T_\epsilon}, and
the bounded cyclic trace identity. The multipliers occur in order
#{\overline f_m,\ldots,\overline f_1,f_1,\ldots,f_m}, with
#{2m} full transfers after adjacent half-powers combine.
Apply it with the present Hilbert space and slice algebra. The
sesquilinear identity for #{F,G} proves independence of the
product-sum presentation, and #{G=F} gives the asserted square.}

\p{For a site reflection, fix #{q_0=x,q_m=z} and define
##{B_F(x,z)=\int F(x,q_1,\ldots,q_{m-1},z)
 \prod_{j=0}^{m-1}k_\epsilon(q_j,q_{j+1})
 \prod_{j=1}^{m-1}dq_j.}
Symmetry of the kernels splits the other half into the conjugate
integral, hence
##{\mathbb E[\overline{F(\theta q)}F(q)]
 =Z_{\epsilon,2m}^{-1}\int|B_F(x,z)|^2\,dx\,dz\ge0.}
The bound #{|B_F|\le\|F\|_\infty k_\epsilon^{(m)}} and the
Hilbert–Schmidt property of #{T_\epsilon^m} give integrability.
For #{m=1}, there is no interior integral and the reflection fixes
both slices.}

\p{Kernel symmetry makes the law reflection invariant. Finite
sums of rectangle indicators are dense in the #{L^2} law of the
positive-half variables, and
##{|\mathbb E[\overline{F\circ\theta}G]|
 \le\|F\|_{L^2(\mu)}\|G\|_{L^2(\mu)}.}
Continuity extends positivity from bounded products to that space.
Polynomial observables belong to it by \ref{fdip-00GX}.}
}

\p{These are the onsite half decompositions of
\ref{fdip-007N} and \ref{fdip-007O} with a whole configuration as
one slice. Each fixed-slice potential is shared equally at a site
cut; a link cut has heat cross terms #{q_vq'_v/\epsilon} with
positive coefficients. On invariant compact cubes the bounded
half decompositions apply, and coercivity supplies a Gaussian
majorant for removing the cutoff. Additional spatial reflection
positivity requires a specified graph reflection and nonnegative
crossing-bond decomposition. An arbitrary finite spatial graph
alone does not supply them.}
